{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2","id":1372943,"title":"Exalted Book Club (Take 2)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-03-05T05:20:35-08:00","id":1372943,"title":"Exalted Book Club (Take 2)","content":"What fiction suggestions for Exalted Inspiration do others have?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12378-fma245","name":"FMA245","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"165"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T05:59:50-08:00","id":1372948,"title":"","content":"Depends on which of its themes you wish to explore. Might I recommend Glen Cook\u2019s <i>Black Company</i> books? Or Tanith Lee\u2019s <i>Night\u2019s Master</i>?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T06:59:25-08:00","id":1372955,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1372948#post1372948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Depends on which of its themes you wish to explore. Might I recommend Glen Cook\u2019s <i>Black Company</i> books? Or Tanith Lee\u2019s <i>Night\u2019s Master</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes you should","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12378-fma245","name":"FMA245","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"165"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T07:24:00-08:00","id":1372960,"title":"","content":"I Second the <i><b>Black Company</b></i> Books... totally awesome <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T09:40:57-08:00","id":1372989,"title":"","content":"Ones I have read that were suggested in books or otherwise I thought were pretty good for exalted:<br /> <br /> <b>Night's Master</b> by Tanith Lee, as well as pretty much the rest of the Tales from the Flat Earth books. It's a suggested reading in all the Exalted coreoboksf or a reason.<br /> <br /> <b>The Black Company</b> by Glenn Cook and all the rest of thsoe there. LIke <b>Night's Master</b> it's one of the books suggested in the 1e corebook.<br /> <br /> <b>Lord of Light</b> by Roger Zelazny for awesome pros, really cool setting, and exotic weird shit. I think i'ts my favorite Zelazny book, which helps it a lot there.<br /> <br /> <b>The Grace of Kings</b> by Kim Liu to me is a really good exmaple of doing a political intrigue fantasy setting that also doesn't have to be entirely existential in its nature. THe follow-up book, <b>Wall of Storms</b> is solid and again, not as existential as it first appears. Although I think it does dirft a bit away from what I would consider good Exalted inspirtaiton exactly since it goes more into the silkpunk aesthetic as it goes along.<br /> <br /> <b>The Gods of Pegana</b> by Lord Dunsany are big inspiration for the setting and generally just cool and interesting. Recommend for good example of the storyteller angle to present the setting.<br /> <br /> <b>The Compleat Traveler in Black</b> by John Brunner is effing weird fantasy. It apparently was a big inspiration for the Sidereal Exalted though, and I think Getimians get a good chunk outta it too. Hard to describe, but look it up and see if it is up your alley.<br /> <br /> <b>The Dying Earth</b> by Jack Vance is pretty old school fantasy, but I think it is from a time where fantasy and sci-fi weren't quite as distinct things as they are today. This is important for Exlated to me since I think a lot of the magitech aesthetic from 2e draws on the idea that they were separate genres in a way I don't tihnk Vance or Zelazny did. It's got some pretty old timey technobabble I find amusing too.<br /> <br /> <b>Shadow of the Torturer </b>by Gene Wolfe and the rest of The Book of New Sun. Really dense, kind of weird, but I think there's actually somer eally cool ideas in there for Exalted like artifacts, the way communiteis are isolated, the long forgotten past of the world, and the nature of the Autarch are all things I think that do well to inspire Exalted stories perosnally.<br /> <br /> <b>Things Fall Apart</b> by Chinua Achebe is a weird one since it's more historical fiction/drama, but I think the big thing to me that stands out is it shows inside the life of some folks in a part of the world not a lot of people see, and I think it illustrates well how people can grow up in a culture with things assumed perfectly normal and with their own issues and failings. And how that can be completely ruined by colonialism, exploitation, and pride. I think really worth reading just to get ideas of such things. The follow-up book <b>No Longer at Ease</b> I think is less relevant to Exalted specifically, but I think is something to think of when deisgning a character that has a lot to them. The book is strongly about what the MC is in context of the start of the story and someone not really knowing or caring the whys of what he did. <br /> <br /> I'll post some of my favorite non-fiction in a bit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T10:42:15-08:00","id":1373001,"title":"","content":"How about for Lunars specifically?<br /> <br /> This one might seem odd at first glance, <a href=\"https://california.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.1525/california/9780520294950.001.0001/upso-9780520294950\" target=\"_blank\">For the wild: ritual and commitment in radical eco-activism</a>, but the way I picture Lunars are something like small direct action groups in the modern world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Lead Writer for the Savage Age of Werewolf: the Apocalypse: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/428750/Ghost-Lineages-The-Advanced-Players-Guide-to-the-Savage-Age\" target=\"_blank\">Ghost Lineages</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/312700/The-Rise-of-the-Garou-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Two\" target=\"_blank\">Rise of the Garou</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/331302/Tools-of-Extinction-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Three\" target=\"_blank\">Tools of Extinction</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/352717/Tribebook-Wlewa\" target=\"_blank\">Tribebook: Wlewa</a>.<br /> <br /> Want RPGs in libraries? Check out my book, <a href=\"http://www.abc-clio.com/ABC-CLIOCorporate/product.aspx?pc=A3951P\" target=\"_blank\">Dragons in the Stacks</a>. Now with <a href=\"http://www.ennie-awards.com/blog/2015-ennie-award-nominees-and-spotlight-winners/\" target=\"_blank\">awards</a>!<br /> \u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","name":"grimjaws","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1813_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T13:45:05-08:00","id":1373061,"title":"","content":"I'm reading Dune right now to prep for a DB game and the upcoming movie and I'd have to say it's a pretty great sample for Dynastic politics. The political maneuvering, the lifestyle you live when your house is in a feuding assassin war with another house, it's good. Also while it is sci-fi things like mentats, and the voice and stuff really suit themselves towards exalted charms and how they might interact.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-05T17:40:56-08:00","id":1373108,"title":"","content":"Seconding both the Gods of Pegana (which is in the public domain so you can read it for free!) and Lord of Light.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-06T07:38:31-08:00","id":1373200,"title":"","content":"If you want the most water aspect-y water aspect, i recommend The Celebrated Cases of Judge Dee.<br /> <br /> A Hero Born, the first book in the Condor Heros wuxia novels finally got an official English translation, too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-06T07:47:06-08:00","id":1373203,"title":"","content":"I love <i>The Journey to the West</i>, but it might be a little hard to penetrate. And a lot of the parts in the middle get rather same-y; there's <i>so many</i> chapters where the travelers come to an inn or a temple or a village <i>or whatever</i> and a bunch of people who seem ordinary are in fact secretly demons who want to eat Tang Sanzang, or seduce him, or trick him into eating human flesh.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T03:53:59-08:00","id":1373350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373203#post1373203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love <i>The Journey to the West</i>, but it might be a little hard to penetrate. And a lot of the parts in the middle get rather same-y; there's <i>so many</i> chapters where the travelers come to an inn or a temple or a village <i>or whatever</i> and a bunch of people who seem ordinary are in fact secretly demons who want to eat Tang Sanzang, or seduce him, or trick him into eating human flesh.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I found Journey to the West very informative for dealing with Lunars, gods, and sidereals--as someone who is often stumped by &quot;well how do shapeshifters ever get caught&quot;. <br /> Lord of Light is good. Tales of the Flat Earth is good. Moorecock is good. I'ma classics nerd, so you know Illiad, Oddessey, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-07T04:47:22-08:00","id":1373356,"title":"","content":"Another vote for Lord of Light-- it's great for the &quot;supernaturally powered beings struggle with each other using the lives of mortals as pawns&quot; side of things. The Chronicles of Amber are another good Zelazny series.<br /> <br /> Brandon Sanderson's Stormlight Archives are good too-- both in the general &quot;these are awesome books&quot; sense, and in that the central plot involves the return of incredibly powerful heroes who used to rule the world and are now seen as monsters.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://killsixbilliondemons.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Kill Six Billion Demons</a> is probably <i>the </i>most Exalted thing I've ever seen, and that's including some Exalted games I've played.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12314-grod_the_giant","name":"Grod_the_giant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12314_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T00:28:08-08:00","id":1373494,"title":"","content":"Kind of a random thing with Lord of Light also to me is the prose. It has just good lines it. I also think Sidhartha is my go-to example of an Integrity Supernal character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T07:19:45-07:00","id":1373543,"title":"","content":"Agreed.<br /> <br /> \u200bMight I also recommend reading the Book of Judges? There's a pretty Exalted-esque depiction in there where a man is <i>such a badass</i> on the battlefield that even God can't kill him - he sends down <i>the stars from the sky</i> to end the guy... and he sweeps them into the water and drowns them. But then a woman's able to do the job, taking him into her tent and then stoving in his temple with a mallet and tent-peg while he sleeps.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 03-08-2020, 02:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T07:47:26-07:00","id":1373548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1372989#post1372989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Shadow of the Torturer </b>by Gene Wolfe and the rest of The Book of New Sun. Really dense, kind of weird, but I think there's actually somer eally cool ideas in there for Exalted like artifacts, the way communiteis are isolated, the long forgotten past of the world, and the nature of the Autarch are all things I think that do well to inspire Exalted stories perosnally.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm currently rereading Book of the New Sun after 17 years or so. In some ways it's harder this time around because I'm actively trying to appreciate what Severian means and learn more about his world when Wolfe wanted definitive answers to be rather difficult.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T08:53:39-07:00","id":1373557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373543#post1373543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Agreed.<br /> <br /> \u200bMight I also recommend reading the Book of Judges? There's a pretty Exalted-esque depiction in there where a man is <i>such a badass</i> on the battlefield that even God can't kill him - he sends down <i>the stars from the sky</i> to end the guy... and he sweeps them into the water and drowns them. But then a woman's able to do the job, seducing him and then stoving in his temple with a mallet and tent-peg while he sleeps.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, Sisera.<br /> Actually, in the &quot;historical&quot; bit, Sisera (general of the Canaanites, ie the lowlanders) takes 900 chariots and an unspecific number of infantry, Barak (who Deborah appointed general of the Israelites, ie the highlanders) takes 10000 warriors.* In the battle, Sisera's army panics. He runs away and goes to hide in a woman called Jael's tent, but she staves his head in with a tent-peg while he's asleep. She doesn't really seduce him though, she just gives him food.<br /> <br /> *Like most pre-modern reports of battles, take the numbers with a pinch of salt.<br /> <br /> The victory song the prophet Deborah sings (which is, incidentally, one of two songs that are by far the oldest parts of the Bible), about how great God and Deborah is (and naming and shaming the tribes who didn't help), does feature earthquakes, rain, stars fighting Sisera, and the torrent Kishon that sweeps &quot;them&quot; away, but I'm not sure what Kishon is or whether &quot;them&quot; is the stars.<br /> <br /> Anyway, nice to see a middle-aged woman famed for her wisdom and intelligence (rather than sexiness or badassery) get to be the leader and hero.<br /> <br /> (Judges has a bit of a post-apocalyptic vibe too, as it's set about 100 years after the Bronze Age Collapse, which they occasionally mention off-hand. Anyway, lots of inspiration for Zeniths. Hmmm... is there a charm for siccing bears on people for mocking your baldness?)<br /> <br /> Moving on.<br /> I quite enjoyed Romance of the Three Kingdoms, because as it's semi-historical, it has a lot more unexpected twists than fictional works do! It's loooong though, and sometimes difficult to keep track of all the characters, especially as some have similar names.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hmmm, what else.<br /> So, ages ago I read a book called Spellwright, by Blake Charlton. To cut a long story short; magic works by glowing runes that you pull out of the air, or people's heads, or something. It's all language based, you literally write the spells. The main character goes to magic school, except that he's got magical dyslexia, and mispells (literally) everything, causing all sorts of chaos. But in the end, while he gets expelled, he learns that his magical dyslexia is essential to the world's magic... for some reason I forget. It's kind of a fun, original-though-it-doesn't-seem-original, okay-not-amazing book. (IIRC, he's dyslexic and his kid is dyslexic so he decided to write dyslexic Harry Potter to inspire his kid, or something like that. Oh, and apparently the author is really nice at conventions.)<br /> <br /> A while later, at the library, I picked up a book called Spellbreaker, which turns out to be the third in a trilogy. It was, again, decent but not amazing.<br /> But anyway, the interesting thing was that it was about a culture chock-full of gods. The main character is the granddaughter of the character from the first book, and her boyfriend is a shark-god from a small group of islands (I think maybe they break up because he can't stop himself knocking island girls up, because the islanders believe he's destined to father a great island hero). She also travels around with a wrestling god as a bodyguard. And they interview a beggar god, do diplomacy with the head god of the island empire, etc, etc.<br /> Previously, I'd always been unsatisfied with gods in Exalted. But this made me realise I should stop using them as dematerialised spirits who hang out in sanctums and their priests do most of the interactions. Instead, I should use them as an excuse to have a wacky character who's weaker than or equal to an Exalt but stronger than a mortal, and I can mostly make up their powers.<br /> So my current game's original sandbox area featured Volundr the strong dwarven volcano-god who the Full Moon competed with in strength (who's also Jarl of the city on that volcano), Orm the irascible snake-god of the harsh river, Orma the art-loving courtier snake-goddess of the pleasant river, Dhrun the falcon-headed god of a mountain who leads a warrior lodge of wrestlers, White Bull the dopy forest god who leads a warrior lodge of minotaurs, and the bay goddess Sig-Yanna the Hungry Maw who dwells in her court of water elementals and mermaids and fish-people in an ancient sunken city. I found that much more satisfying. I think one of them dematerialised, once (after a PC beat him in a menhir-lobbing contest).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I have one more book (series) I'd be tempted to recommend. It contains at least 5 really enjoyable books!<br /> It's about a young man who is the reincarnation of an ancient hero from a lost age of advanced magic and magitek. He wields immense power, but this power is tainted with madness. As his power's grow, he must contend with the invasive memories of his past life, a great hero who went steadily insane.<br /> The protagonist's best friends are a guy who gains the memories of history's greatest generals, and a dream-walking wolf-eyed commander of wolves with amazing senses and reflexes like a wolf.<br /> However, our protagonist must contend with two enemies;<br /> 1) the continent's main religion, whose magic nuns believe that his madness-tainted power cannot be allowed to threaten the world and so he must be killed or have his magic taken away;<br /> 2) 13 ancient beings, the Dreadlords, who are also heroes of the ancient lost age but ones who swore themselves to an ancient bound evil in return for great power.<br /> <br /> But, well... as they say, friends don't let friends read the Wheel of Time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 03-08-2020, 11:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T09:22:58-07:00","id":1373561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373494#post1373494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kind of a random thing with Lord of Light also to me is the prose. It has just good lines it. I also think Sidhartha is my go-to example of an Integrity Supernal character.</div> </div> </div> </div>And the most infamous pun in the history of Fantasy. George Martin has an interesting story about it in the foreword, sheds some light on both writers attitude to creation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24379-hoardandsue","name":"HoardAndSue","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T11:37:16-07:00","id":1373579,"title":"","content":"I'm gonna toss out a recommendation for <b>Debt: The First 5000 Years</b>. It's definitely not a narrative text, but it's incredibly informative and is packed with the history and culture of civilizations all throughout earth's history. The book's presentation and style are also extremely polished. I found it a really fun read.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11619-kymmetheseventh","name":"KymmetheSeventh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T12:08:46-07:00","id":1373584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HoardAndSue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373561#post1373561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And the most infamous pun in the history of Fantasy. George Martin has an interesting story about it in the foreword, sheds some light on both writers attitude to creation.</div> </div> </div> </div>What was it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T12:21:49-07:00","id":1373587,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373584#post1373584\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What was it?</div> </div> </div> </div><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">There's this bit where Siddhartha goes to have a new body made for him, but isn't convinced he'll pass the mind-probe, so he negotiates with Heaven to have them skip it. Then, still unsure, he brings in someone else, a <i>shan</i>, to pose as him and get the new body. Per his suspicions, the new body made for &quot;Siddhartha&quot; has epilepsy.<br /> <br /> \u200bHe sees this when <b>the fit</b> hits <b>the shan.</b></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-08T14:31:52-07:00","id":1373616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KymmetheSeventh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373579#post1373579\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm gonna toss out a recommendation for <b>Debt: The First 5000 Years</b>. It's definitely not a narrative text, but it's incredibly informative and is packed with the history and culture of civilizations all throughout earth's history. The book's presentation and style are also extremely polished. I found it a really fun read.</div> </div> </div> </div>Reminds me, some good non-fiction for Exalted:<br /> <br /> <b>Debt: the First 5,000 Years</b> by David Graeber, as mentioned. A lot of Exalted 3e's stuff on economics and how the Resources Merit even like, work, take inspiration from this one. It has good examples of how different cultures did things, how different societies handled debt and usery, and so on. Lots of good dieas to yoink from for your own games.<br /> <br /> <b>1491: New Revelations of the World Before Columbus</b> by Charles C. Mann is interesting for explorations of the pre-Columbian New World, the kinds of socieites there, notable societies, and the impacts of the European conquest in the realms of disease and war. I think the stuff on the post-disease years is actually pretty good idea also of the way Creation probably felt like a lot. And also there's some interesting bits on the way that colonial powers at times view conquered peoples, which has its own interesting elements when considering the Realm.<br /> <br /> <b>1177 BC: the Year Civilization Collapsed</b> by Eric H. Cline is really cool. Creation is often called Bronze Age, but I think that folks actually 1) Assume that's more primative htan it is and 2) Miss out on a lot of the stuff that is later. But still, it is a great book on giving a snapshot of the world of the Bronze Age, how interconnected it was, the way war was conducted, and so on. The causes of its collapse and such are also probably good inspriations for the current Time of Tumult and what the Realm Civil War might do it things.<br /> <br /> <b>Seeing Like a State</b> by James C. Scott is kind of a different on. It's about mostly the 20th Century, but the previous devs at least have noted that a lot of how it talks about Modernist thought, and the human costs of some of how it was implemented were really itneresting. I think it's a good example to me on what kinds of things to consider when building &quot;large scale&quot; stories, and how often drama isn't about whether or not you succeed, but whether you had unforseen consequences when you get your policies implemented. Exalted rulers have often a lot of these same conditions of the things the book talks about, and I htink it's ag ood book for both STs and players to consider on building such stories.<br /> <br /> <b>Against the Grain</b> also by James C. Scott is insteada bout how agriculture came about, what led to folks building cities, and even some interesting points on how for a lot of human history, non-settled peoples were the majority. I think it's especially interesting in context of how the Realm and Threshold probably see &quot;barbarians&quot; and how a lot of the space between settlements have vibrant, healthy cultures. Plus the kind sof means states do to create themselves, exert power, and maintain power over their populations.<br /> <br /> <b>King Leopold's Ghost </b>by Adam Hochschild is an account of how brutal extractional colonialism and empire can get. Kind of wroth remembering that a chunk of the Realm's inspiration is not just be The Empire in a fantasy sense. It's The Empire running on real politik. And that means the Realm evil doesn't have to ham things up. Real life empire was brutal as it was.<br /> <br /> <b>Zealot: the Life and Times of Jesus of Nazareth</b> by Raza Aslan is pretty good at painting a picture of occupied Judeah in the 1st Century. I like how it discusses things like the Roman views of the province, the people there's views of the Romans, and what a historical Jesus might have been like at the time, how he rose to power an dhow some accidents of history led to the version of the Christian faith we hae today. I think that it's good for imagining the Realm's occupaiton of a place, how quickly religions can build up, and the impact people have over time, and how quickly faiths can develop. (This last one is important to me in understnading how fast the Immaculate Philosophy spread, and any modern groups like the Cult of the Illumianted might operate.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-14T06:28:24-07:00","id":1374748,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1372943-exalted-book-club-take-2?p=1373557#post1373557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have one more book (series) I'd be tempted to recommend. It contains at least 5 really enjoyable books!<br /> It's about a young man who is the reincarnation of an ancient hero from a lost age of advanced magic and magitek. He wields immense power, but this power is tainted with madness. As his power's grow, he must contend with the invasive memories of his past life, a great hero who went steadily insane.<br /> The protagonist's best friends are a guy who gains the memories of history's greatest generals, and a dream-walking wolf-eyed commander of wolves with amazing senses and reflexes like a wolf.<br /> However, our protagonist must contend with two enemies;<br /> 1) the continent's main religion, whose magic nuns believe that his madness-tainted power cannot be allowed to threaten the world and so he must be killed or have his magic taken away;<br /> 2) 13 ancient beings, the Dreadlords, who are also heroes of the ancient lost age but ones who swore themselves to an ancient bound evil in return for great power.<br /> <br /> But, well... as they say, friends don't let friends read the Wheel of Time.</div> </div> </div> </div>*clap clap clap*<br /> <br /> This has to be the best &quot;advertisement text&quot; i have ever seen for WoT. It almost makes me want to start it again (got stuck on book...11, I think). Well done!<br /> <br /> Not sure how Exalted-y WoT is, though. Pretty good fantasy-fare? For sure. At least in the beginning. Jordan really knew how to create a world out of a lot of real-world pieces patched together in new ways.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25439-mnemon-liranan","name":"Mnemon Liranan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25439_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-14T07:10:18-07:00","id":1374752,"title":"","content":"The reincarnation-related stuff, and the First Age, is quite Exalted. The magic system isn't. Other than the Dreadlords, the bad guys are more like Warhammer: beastmen from the Chaos Wastes to the north.<br /> <br /> If you got stuck at 11, I'd recommend reading the last 3... ie the ones written by Sanderson instead of Jordan. They're much faster-paced, as you generally get from Sanderson. 5-11 are the really slog-fest ones.<br /> <br /> Which reminds me.<br /> The Stormlight Archive, by Brandon Sanderson, is set in an exotic world of rock and crustaceans and massive storms. A long time ago, magical reincarnating warriors armed with huge (but light) armour and weapons, empowered by their principles and ideals, fought to save the world. But they abandoned their duties, turned from good, and vanished. <br /> In the modern day, aristocrats use the ancient magical arms and armour (the giant swords can be summoned and dismissed into elsewhere). But now, the ancient warriors are being reborn, bringing back secret magic from long ago.<br /> The fights in that feel much more Exalted. Search &quot;chasmfiend&quot;, &quot;shardblade&quot; or &quot;shardplate&quot; on Google images, you'll see what I mean.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-16T02:03:43-07:00","id":1375039,"title":"","content":"Upon a Burning Throne, book 1 of the Burnt Empires Saga, by Ashok K. Banker. It's not precisely the legends and folklore of the Hindu religion, but it is <i>heavily </i>based on them. It's full of high-level demigods, and the villain Jarsun can do a lot of weird demon-y things that I would love 3E Infernals to be able to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>She/Her pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12975-zealot_vedas","name":"Zealot_Vedas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12975_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"85"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}